!PC 3.09 update
===============

This updates an installed !PC 3.08 to 3.09.  It holds only the files that
have changed, in a !PC directory of their own: copy it over your existing
!PC so that they replace the old ones.  Back up your !PC first.

  !PC.!RunImage                 the application
  !PC.PCBits.Core.PCMem         the memory manager module
  !PC.PCBits.Netlinks           networking for the PC (new): PCNAT, PCNE2,
                                Startup and Shutdown

What's new
----------

- PCMem's dynamic area handler is C now, through a cmhg veneer.  3.08's
  wrote two flags to the wrong place in the RMA, which could put a page of
  video RAM in !PC's slot and crash it.  Seen on the first start of !PC
  after the machine started.

- The screen area is grown before the full-screen mode is set.

- MiddleToPC: with "MiddleToPC On" in a PC configuration, the middle
  mouse button goes to the PC; Ctrl+middle still gives !PC's menu.

- Mouse port 0x31B, command 9, latches where the ARM's pointer is, in the
  PC's pixels, for the PC to read at 0x318-0x31B.

- PCNAT, a network interface for the PC with no card behind it.  The PC
  gets a private network behind RISC OS, by DHCP: it is 10.0.2.15, the
  gateway is 10.0.2.2 and DNS is 10.0.2.3, which passes queries on to the
  first of RISC OS's Inet$Resolvers.  Each connection the PC makes becomes
  a socket of RISC OS's own, so RISC OS keeps its address and TCP/IP
  (sharing a card with the PC through PCNE2 alone can't do that).

Networking
----------

PCNAT needs the Internet 5 module and the Mbuf manager, and RISC OS's own
TCP/IP set up and working.  PCBits.Netlinks loads PCNAT and then PCNE2,
which gives the PC an NE2000 (its IRQ is the configuration's NE2000Int).
In the PC configuration use

  NE2000Basic -pTCPIP

PCNE2 uses PCNAT's interface, pcnat0, unless Diva$SuggestedInterface or a
-D option names another: to put the PC on a network card of its own as
before, set Diva$SuggestedInterface to that card's interface.

*PCNATInfo says what PCNAT is doing.

PCNAT's SWI chunk (&5A4C0) is a development one, not yet allocated.
